accinated  and has had another two or three weeks  to acquire immunity  and another three or four weeks until  we've crunched all the data  do you uh have a view then on on where  the government could move more quickly  out of locked lockdown and move more  quickly  towards at least a semblance of  normality what should it be doing  in your uh personal view that it's not  doing at the moment  well i think we could be moving much  faster once the  first phase of the vaccination program  is completed  somewhere around in the second half of  april by that time we've taken out  98 percent of the mortality risk almost  as much of the  severe illness risk there's a little bit  more in the  under 50 in the fort over 40s the 40 to  50 year olds  but i mean that could be marked up  fairly quickly  so we're then saying well why would we  lock up a low-risk  population for the summer rather than  thinking about  the maximum number of restrictions that  we can remove  early in may you might say well it's  only  six or eight weeks coming forward but  those six or eight weeks  are going to make a great difference to  the economy they're going to be  an opportunity for the health service to  get going on the  catching up with the backlog of elective  surgery at a time when  business is getting quieter there's a  chance for the  overstressed staff of the health service  to you know get a little bit of space to  relax and recover  and i really don't see the justification  for  postponing everything in way  way into the summer when  there is obviously one run small risks  but there are tolerable risks of  moving much more quickly so what did you  make of the sage  warning the famous sage advisory body to  the government with neil ferguson  probably the most famous member of it on  that saying this week that if you ease  too much before easter it could lead to  55 000 more deaths they projected  what do you say when you see something  like that well i think it's very hard to  see where these numbers are coming from  it the problem with models is always  that they're only as good as the data  that you put into them  and the assumptions you make to you to  join that data up  and the the modeling that we've seen so  far  it's made unrealistic assumptions about  the uptake of the vaccination and the  effectiveness of the vaccination  and the models haven't really been  corrected to  to allow for that it's made unrealistic  assumptions about fatality rates  which have been falling with the  improvements in treatment since  since last year and  you can play with the numbers you can  produce a reasonable worse case  but we tend not to hear what's the  reasonable best case  and since we're heading down towards you  know maybe a hundred deaths a day from  covid within the the next couple of  weeks  it's very hard to see where all those  excess deaths are going to come from  do you see any sign in the government's  announcements and in its deliberations  that  it could take this view that it it  begins down the  the path that it has already explained  but it said it looks and says hey this  is actually going better than we thought  um we could speed this up a bit is there  any chance that could happen  well i think there are certain  opportunities for that i mean if the  you know if the lines on the graph  continue in the same direction for  another two or three weeks  i would imagine that some conservative  mps will be asking very serious  questions about  whether they should vote for the renewal  of some of the powers at the end of  march  and i but i think the position is quite  clear that  the government will only move faster if  there are people pushing them to do so  um and the people pushing them to do so  will be pushing against some very  formidable obstacles  uh in terms of you know the risk  aversion of some ministers the risk  aversion some scientists  um and i i think this sort of  sense that you know the political elite  the scientifically the countries they  lead  have really been very comfortable you  know we could all work from home  you know we've got big houses and we've  got gardens and we're really  disconnected from the lives of the  the people who are serving us the people  who are doing the deliveries  working in the shops working the food  processing plants  um and you know they deserve to have  their lives thought about too  and what it would take for you know for  them to be  released from the the sort of  confinement that that we've  we've imposed over the last year or so  it's certainly true that those coming up  with the confinement  and not those who bear the brunt of that  confirmed confinement i think that's  true in the political and the scientific  elite and the media  elite as well they're they're all not  bearing the brunt of it  my guess would be that the prime  minister's instincts would be  to listen to you rather than to other  experts  but he's overwhelmed overcome  by what i would call the precautionary  principle that as a politician  is better he is over cautious  than takes what might be seen as a bit  of a gamble with  the kind of advice you would give well i  think it's one of the interesting things  andrew is that that phrase precautionary  principle has been used so incorrectly  all the way through  i mean when it was originally coined in  the 1990s  i i was doing work on this in connection  with genetically modified crops  the precautionary principle says  basically you don't do  stuff unless you've got a very thorough  appraisal  of the potential harms as well as the  potential benefits  and all the way through we've really  failed to give as much attention to the  harms of policies  as we have to the uh alleged benefits  so we've never really had this this sort  of balanced  look at what is happening to society  to the economy to the mental health of  the people  as a result of the various control  measures  now in part that's because the whole  sage network was set up  with a single issue focus you know  stop covid and you the  people involved with it are not asked to  consider the wider impact of their  actions  and i think that that has skewed the  advice coming to government  it's meant that there's been perhaps  less open debate  than there should have been within the  scientific community as much as within  the political community  and that the result of that is this sort  of drift  towards you know  if the objective is curbing covered at  all costs  then in a sense all costs become  justified  and what people like me i suppose are  trained to do  as sociologists is to put covid  into a context to say that there's a  societal pandemic here as well as a  biomedical pandemic  and that societal pandemic looks a bit  different and it  should point us towards a rather  different approach to the policies  which is which which has a more perhaps  a better balance between the intended  and the unintended consequences  so it's what we in economics would call  a full cost benefit analysis  of the whole policy professor we'll  leave it there i know you've got to get  away you have a busy  day uh we thank you for putting your  your case so coherently  and giving our viewers something to  think about different from  what they're hearing from government  professor ding well there thank you for  being  on spectator tv  [Music]  you

